description:
engine/hexen2/world.c:482-507 special-cases any zero-sized entity whose classname CONTAINS "puzzle" and rewrites its absmin/absmax to a -8 -8 -28 / 8 8 8 box. Introduced by 8ca3be8bb, narrowed from v.mins/v.maxs to absmin/absmax by c86f09b14 after it destroyed map items (uhexen2-4p8t).

It should be deleted outright. Three findings, all verified:

1. THE PREMISE IS WRONG. The workaround exists because uhexen2-ogsd concluded puzzle pieces stay zero-sized forever, reading PlaceItem only as far as "setsize (self, self.mins,self.maxs)" at gamecode/hc/h2/items.hc:58. Twenty lines below, PlaceItem sets the real box itself, right after droptofloor:

     if(self.touch==puzzle_touch)
     {
         setorigin(self,self.origin+"0 0 28");
         setsize (self, "-8 -8 -28", "8 8 8");   // items.hc:76-80
     }

   So every non-FLOATING puzzle piece ends up correctly sized from the progs. The workaround is inert for them -- including the Dark Pharaoh Scepter (egypt5, spawnflags 32768 = normal path) that motivated ogsd, and SoT docks Green Key.

2. IT IS NOT LOAD-BEARING FOR THE REST EITHER. Scanned every installed map set (data1, portals, sot, so5, ros, karma2): 123 puzzle* entities, of which 87 normal and 36 FLOATING. FLOATING pieces (map spawnflag 2, remapped to spawnflags=1 by puzzle_piece() in triggers.hc) take PlaceItem s else branch, which sets only movetype = MOVETYPE_NONE and never calls setsize -- their box does stay zero. But they remain pickup-able in vanilla because FL_ITEM already expands absmin/absmax by +/-15 in X and Y at world.c:513-519, leaving a flat z-plane that overlaps the player s vertical extent. That is how retail shipped and how Portals is completable. On those 36 the workaround is not repairing a break, it is silently making pickup more forgiving than vanilla.

   FLOATING pieces in shipped maps: portals keep1 (orb2), keep3 (scroll), keep5 (crosskey), tibet4 (prayer2), tibet6 (greengem), and others.

3. THE MATCH IS A COMPATIBILITY HAZARD. strstr(classname, "puzzle") also catches puzzle_static_piece and any mod entity class containing "puzzle" that legitimately links with a zero-size box, silently resizing it. Raised by Spoike (FTEQW): engine-side manipulation of entity sizes is not a workaround, it is a compatibility nightmare. He is right, and it already cost us the SoT Green Key once.

The verification task uhexen2-y6r1 was closed 2026-06-18 without ever being run ("no external reporter, self-filed reminder. Collision fix already landed"), so this was never confirmed necessary -- only assumed.

design:
FIX: delete the dead-by-intent, destructive-in-fact block in the bag branch.

  h2      items.hc:1730-1734
  portals items.hc:1753-1757
  hw      items.hc:2561-2565

    if (!total)
    {
        remove(item);
        return;
    }

Reaching the bag branch already implies something is being dropped: the genuine nothing-to-drop case returned at the early-out (h2 items.hc:1571-1575, which tests total AND all three instant items). The only way to arrive at the bag branch with total==0 is a mana/health-only drop -- precisely the case that must survive. So the block can never fire for a legitimate reason.

WHY NOT COPY SIEGE VERBATIM: siege sets total=TRUE right after its early-out (items.hc:2381-2382), which is safe there ONLY because siege has its entire single-item gate commented out (items.hc:2394-2515) and always takes the bag branch. In h2/portals/hw the gate at items.hc:1586-1589 tests "total == 1" and sits AFTER the early-out, so forcing total=TRUE would misroute mana/health-only drops into the single-item dispatch -- a blue+health drop would spawn the mana and silently lose the health. Do not do this.

Leave siege unchanged.

VERIFY: all four trees compile with hcc from .#utils (h2 needs both progs.dat and progs2.dat).

description:
ASYMMETRY BETWEEN GRANT AND ROLLBACK. Confirmed by code reading, not theory.

GRANT (engine/h2shared/pr_cmds.c:490-494), when a misc_modelpimp carries flags with EF_HOLEY (16384):
    mod->flags = mod->orig_flags | (new_flags & 0x01ffffff);
    if ((mod->flags & EF_HOLEY) && !(mod->orig_flags & EF_HOLEY))
        Mod_ReuploadAliasSkins(mod);
Mod_ReuploadAliasSkins re-runs Mod_LoadAllSkins with the granted flags, so the skins are re-uploaded through the TEX_HOLEY path in GL_Upload8 -- every palette-index-0 texel becomes alpha 0. Note this writes the SHARED qmodel_t, so it affects every entity using that model.

ROLLBACK (engine/h2shared/gl_model.c, Mod_RestoreAliasModelDefaults), called from R_NewMap on map change:
    mod->flags = mod->orig_flags;
    mod->ex_flags = mod->orig_ex_flags;
    memcpy(mod->glow_settings, ...);
    Mod_SetExtraFlags(mod);
It restores the flags and NEVER re-uploads the skins.

CONSEQUENCE. A model pimped EF_HOLEY on map A carries HOLEY skin alpha into map B while mod->flags says opaque. R_DrawAliasModel then takes the opaque branch with u_alpha_threshold = 0.01 -- but the alpha test is UNCONDITIONAL (see the khsa r20 comment in salias_frag, gl_shader.c: r15 gated the discard on threshold > 0.5 and that was reverted because opaque-path models legitimately carry cutout texels). alpha 0 < 0.01, so discard STILL FIRES on every index-0 texel. The model renders with holes punched at its index-0 texels using an otherwise-correct texture, and mip/anisotropic filtering makes the boundary texels flicker across the threshold as the view angle changes.

This survives only while the model is not purged on the transition; the purge is gated on flush_textures && gl_purge_maptex.

SoT maps that grant EF_HOLEY via misc_modelpimp: Arena (empty2, empty4), cathedral (book), courtyard (parchment, empty4), demo1 (empty4), docks/dungeon/egypt/egypt2/keep/roman/winter (parchment), keep+tibet (book), winter (empty).

DONE: the rollback re-uploads skins for any model whose EF_HOLEY/EF_TRANSPARENT/EF_SPECIAL_TRANS state actually changes during the restore, so skin alpha and mod->flags cannot disagree across a map change. Add a regression check that loads a map granting EF_HOLEY, changes to one that does not, and asserts the model's skin alpha is opaque again.

design:
Make directory enumeration re-entrant rather than locking around it — a mutex would serialise the worker's file I/O against main-thread menu operations and reintroduce the frame hitch async save exists to remove.

Replace the global find API with a caller-owned context in engine/h2shared/sys.h:

  typedef struct { void *priv; char name[MAX_OSPATH]; } fsfind_t;
  const char *Sys_FindFirstFile (fsfind_t *ctx, const char *path, const char *pattern);
  const char *Sys_FindNextFile (fsfind_t *ctx);
  void Sys_FindClose (fsfind_t *ctx);

priv points at a malloc'd platform struct (HANDLE + WIN32_FIND_DATAW on Windows; DIR* + path + pattern on Unix) — malloc, not Z_Malloc, because it must be callable off-thread. The UTF-8 result goes in ctx->name, removing the shared Sys_WideToUTF8 static. Unix builds its stat path into a local buffer instead of va(). This also retires the Sys_Error-on-reentry landmine.

Update all five call sites: host.c:115 and :147, server/host.c:90 and :123, quakefs.c:1280. Four sys implementations change: hexen2/sys_win.c, hexen2/sys_unix.c, server/sys_win.c, server/sys_unix.c.

Separately, drop the zone from FS_CopyFile and FS_WriteFileFromHandle: FS_CreatePath only needs a mutable MAX_OSPATH-bounded copy, so use a stack buffer with a truncation check.

description:
Two console/input-focus items from Mathuzzz, Discord 2026-08-23, both re-confirmed against the latest build:
  * 'console won't close'
  * 'you cannot write into the console in the menu after it gave you some message'

Reported in the same breath as the map-not-found path — on 2026-08-21 he said 'the console no longer hangs when couldn't find the map, but the other issues I mentioned remain', so these two are what is left of that cluster and are probably reachable the same way: try to load a map that does not exist, then try to use the console.

LEAD FOR 'WON'T CLOSE' — Con_ToggleConsole_f, engine/hexen2/console.c:84:
    if (dest == key_console || (dest == key_game && con_forcedup)) {
        if (cls.state == ca_active) Key_SetDest (key_game);
        else                        M_Menu_Main_f ();
    } else Key_SetDest (key_console);
Closing the console requires somewhere to close TO. After a failed map load cls.state is not ca_active and con_forcedup is true (gl_screen.c:1033/1035 set it from !cl.worldmodel || cls.signon != SIGNONS), so the close branch lands in M_Menu_Main_f, and toggling again from key_menu falls to the else and reopens the console. Console <-> main menu, with no way back to a game because there is no game. Whether that reads as 'won't close' depends on what he expects to see; needs his repro.

LEAD FOR 'CANNOT WRITE INTO IT' — the key_dest routing in Key_Event (keys.c:1486) has five destinations and only key_game/key_console reach Key_Console. Anything that parks key_dest in key_message (Con_MessageMode_f, console.c:149/162) or key_menubind (menu.c:4989) silently eats every keystroke the console would otherwise get. key_menubind looks correctly balanced — M_Keybind always ends Key_SetDest(key_menu) except a deliberate early return while a gamepad alt-modifier is held — so key_message is the better suspect, as is any menu path that sets a dest and returns early. Note M_Keybind also swallows a backtick (it is in the key != K_ESCAPE && key != '`' cancel test), so the console key does nothing while a bind is being captured.

NEEDED FROM HIM — these are interactive state bugs and code reading has taken them as far as it usefully goes:
  1. exact steps: which message, from where (menu? console? a failed map load?), and what he presses
  2. is a map loaded at the time, or is he at the main menu with no game running?
  3. does ESC get him out, or is the whole input dead?
  4. what 'some message' says verbatim — that identifies which code path put it on screen.

Mathuzzz, Discord 2026-08-23: 'modelpimp give the models glow even if they don't have it chosen'. Restated from 2026-08-21: 'it now makes the rotation as it should when using spawnflag 32, but it still creates the glow even without the spawnflag 4, which was supposed to be required for the glow'. Re-confirmed against the latest build, so bde39a39c (stop inferring a glow orb from colour on r15-era misc_modelpimps) did not close it.

There are TWO independent routes to a glow with no PIMP_SF_GLOW, and bde39a39c only narrowed one of them.

ROUTE 1 — propagation onto the SHARED MODEL. pr_cmds.c:646:
    if (!(spawnflags & PIMP_SF_SELFONLY)) {
        mod->ex_flags |= (pimp->ex_flags & (EF_GLOW | EF_ILLUMINATE | XF_NO_DLIGHT));
        if (pimp->ex_flags & (EF_GLOW | EF_ILLUMINATE))
            memcpy(mod->glow_settings, pimp->glow_settings, sizeof(mod->glow_settings));
    }
This writes onto qmodel_t, not onto the entity. R_GetPimpFlags (gl_rmain.c:4235) then returns
    pimp_overrides[entnum].ex_flags | e->model->ex_flags
for EVERY entity, so one misc_modelpimp that does set spawnflag 4 turns the glow on for every other entity sharing that model — including entities that set no glow spawnflag at all. That is precisely 'models glow even if they don't have it chosen'. It is deliberate (uhexen2-oq0a, SoT-style mods where a hidden controller drives siblings) and the opt-out is PIMP_SF_SELFONLY, but a modder placing several pimps against one model gets no warning that one of them is speaking for all of them.

ROUTE 2 — the legacy colour inference, pr_cmds.c ~610, still live:
    if (color_set && !(spawnflags & (PIMP_SF_GLOW | PIMP_SF_ILLUMINATE))
                  && !(spawnflags & PIMP_SF_R15_MASK))
        pimp->ex_flags |= EF_GLOW;
A colour with no glow spawnflag and none of the r15 reach flags (16/32/64) still infers an orb. bde39a39c exempted r15-era entities only. Any entity of his that sets a colour and stays on legacy spawnflags keeps getting the orb.

ASK HIM, since these are distinguishable from the map:
  * do the offending misc_models share a model with a DIFFERENT misc_modelpimp that does set spawnflag 4 or 128? -> Route 1, fix is PIMP_SF_SELFONLY on the controller, or a per-entity rather than per-model grant
  * or does the pimp set a colour and use only legacy spawnflags? -> Route 2
  * 'mcache' now reports the engine-assigned group (TORCH_GLOW / GLOW / MISSILE_GLOW / ILLUMINATE / NO_DLIGHT) per 8b29be928 — ask him to paste it, it distinguishes the two routes directly.

Route 1 is also the mechanism by which XF_NO_DLIGHT leaks model-wide, which is worth keeping in mind while working uhexen2-olku.

GL_Shaders_Init unconditionally builds the OIT variants:

    GL_InitOITProgram(&gl_shader_world_oit,    ...);
    GL_InitOITProgram(&gl_shader_alias_oit,    ...);
    GL_InitOITProgram(&gl_shader_particle_oit, ...);

On the ES tier none of them can ever be selected. Every call site picks between the OIT and non-OIT program with 'OIT_InPass() ? &gl_shader_X_oit : &gl_shader_X' (gl_rsurf.c:570/:599/:620/:638, gl_rmain.c:697/:873/:914, r_part.c:1545), and oit_in_pass is only set inside OIT_BeginTranslucency, which returns early unless HW_OIT_HAS_BLEND_FUNCI. That macro is a literal 0 under USE_GLES because glBlendFunci is GL 4.0 and WebGL2 does not expose per-draw-buffer blend equations at all. So OIT_InPass() is permanently false there and the three programs are dead weight: six shader compiles and three links at every startup, on the tier where startup cost and memory matter most.

The resolve side already gets this right — GL_PostProcess_Init guards OIT_InitShader() on the same HW_OIT_HAS_BLEND_FUNCI condition, so the resolve programs are correctly not built on ES. Only gl_shader.c's three variants missed the memo.

## Correction this carries
uhexen2-g63k fixed a real bug (the OIT preamble was spliced ahead of the GLSL ES precision qualifier, so all three failed to compile on ES) but its write-up framed the consequence as 'no order-independent translucency on the tier at all', which reads as though fixing it restores OIT there. It does not. OIT is independently and permanently unavailable on ES for the glBlendFunci reason above. What g63k actually bought is the removal of three guaranteed compile failures and their console noise on every ES startup — worth having, and the fix is still correct for its own sake, but it is not 'OIT now works in the browser'. Anyone reading g63k should read this bead too.

## Fix
Guard the three GL_InitOITProgram calls the same way OIT_InitShader is guarded. Call sites are already safe with a zero program: the ternaries never choose the OIT branch, gl_rmain.c:4229 tests .program before touching it, and GL_Shaders_Shutdown skips zeros.

Commit a14b5eecd (the squashed "SDL3/GL4.3 modernization" commit) expanded DropBackpack's error reporting in h2/hw/portals/siege items.hc:

Was:
  dprint("Bad backpack!")

Now:
  dprint("Bad backpack! ");
  dprint(self.classname);
  dprint(" at ");
  dprint(vtos(self.origin));
  dprint("\n");

This is behind a dprint gate so the impact is cosmetic (debug-only), but it is fork gamecode that nobody decided on — the commit message never mentions it. Once bundling lands, this diagnostic will ship to every player running our progs.dat.

Two choices:
1. KEEP: Document it in gamecode/README as diagnostic instrumentation (and relate to uhexen2-9gdx, which specifically wants improved debugging output)
2. TRIM: Remove it and keep diagnostic output for developer builds only

Related:
- uhexen2-8zia (audit that classified this as category (c) — undecided fork change)
- uhexen2-9gdx (wants "improved/robust error handling and diagnostic output" in badpack fallthrough)
